{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bug was using `int(p / 100 * n)` as a 0-based index, which is one rank too high for nearest-rank (e.g. p50 on 1..10 gave index 5 → 6 instead of rank 5 → 5) and blew up at p=100 when `int(n)` was out of range.\n\nNearest-rank uses a 1-based rank `max(1, ceil(p/100 × n))` and returns `ordered[rank - 1]`, so p=0 maps to rank 1 (minimum), p=100 to rank n (maximum), and boundaries stay in range. Visible tests pass."}]},"session_id":"948e6390-475b-4ef8-9041-8aa255e585d8"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":49054,"duration_api_ms":49054,"is_error":false,"result":"Inspecting the percentile implementation and tests.\n\n\nUsing nearest-rank: rank = max(1, ceil(p/100 × n)), then return the value at that 1-based rank.\n\n\nThe bug was using `int(p / 100 * n)` as a 0-based index, which is one rank too high for nearest-rank (e.g. p50 on 1..10 gave index 5 → 6 instead of rank 5 → 5) and blew up at p=100 when `int(n)` was out of range.\n\nNearest-rank uses a 1-based rank `max(1, ceil(p/100 × n))` and returns `ordered[rank - 1]`, so p=0 maps to rank 1 (minimum), p=100 to rank n (maximum), and boundaries stay in range. Visible tests pass.","session_id":"948e6390-475b-4ef8-9041-8aa255e585d8","request_id":"2889730e-e4cb-4787-9081-7352307a75b7","usage":{"inputTokens":13292,"outputTokens":2350,"cacheReadTokens":79763,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
